#{[17592186162114 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162114 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162114 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Conditional PDF" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162114 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162114 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eb3b4b6a-3a24-5079-bf33-ed1c5576ffa7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1045 "Variance of Sample Means" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186163068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162169 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fdff7ccc-5a47-5748-b28e-bcf8de0682a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162176 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162176 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162176 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162176 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162176 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162176 #edb/ref 1045 "Conditional Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162176 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186163007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162176 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162176 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fa26629c-b4dd-57db-9661-3b43692ea236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162390 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143459" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162390 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162390 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\,0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d20c523-ad94-5c8f-adf5-6fe709391a09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162390 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{x-1}^{1-x}1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y\\,{|}_{x-1}^{1-x} \\\\  & =(1-x)-(x-1) \\\\  & =2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2-2x, & \\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2-2x}, & \\,0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163007 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163007 #edb/ref 1061 34590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163007 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc469a65-06a8-5cb9-9eed-1c3d9f00c4c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163007 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163068 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163068 #edb/ref 1061 38034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163068 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0fb9bf51-5dff-5207-8c2c-54d18ba6afd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163068 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163069 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163069 #edb/ref 1061 38036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163069 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0828c240-f63f-5c50-a2e8-eba11979ee4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163069 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163070 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163070 #edb/ref 1061 38037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163070 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ae3ab16a-d830-544c-8331-c62d1505d7dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163070 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163071 #edb/ref 1061 38040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163071 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c760552-8a9d-54b8-962b-87e6f849c3da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163071 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163078 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163078 #edb/ref 1061 38091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163078 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d645e06-ba3d-5245-bff3-396e4a4a04e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163078 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163079 #edb/ref 1061 38092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163079 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f08e3c7b-ff47-5108-9d4d-3471aa625605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163079 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163080 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163080 #edb/ref 1061 38093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163080 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ccc00629-902d-501f-86c2-7072c1aad6a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163080 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1061 38149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f745e7f-72c3-508a-96ad-ef71ea659d96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1061 38150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c79d4341-23ea-55d9-84aa-e073291790d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163291 #edb/ref 1064 "We can easily extend the notion of conditional probability distributions for discrete random variables to continuous random variables.\n\nFor continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$, the **conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y = y$** is given by\n\n$$\nf_{X \\mid Y} (x \\mid y) =\\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_Y (y)}, \\qquad f_Y(y) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nand the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X = x$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{Y \\mid X} (y \\mid x) =\\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_X (x)}, \\qquad f_X(x) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nNotice that these definitions are analogous to those for discrete random variables.\n\nTo compute probabilities, we work directly with these conditional PDFs. For example, to calculate the **conditional probability of $X$ given $Y$,** we use\n\n$$\nP(X \\in A \\mid Y = y) =\\int_A f_{X \\mid Y} (x \\mid y)\\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\nIn general, the conditional distribution of $X$ given $Y$ does not equal the conditional distribution of $Y$ given $X$. That is\n\n$$\nf_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y) \\ne f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)\n$$\n\nFinally, if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\nf_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)=f_X(x),\\qquad f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)=f_Y(y)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163291 #edb/ref 1065 5625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163291 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4e66c559-ad43-5152-a04e-7aa8b01374ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163291 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the sample mean, denoted $\\overline{X},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is a random variable whose probability distribution (also called the **sampling distribution**) has an expected value of $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nLet's now find the variance of the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ Using the properties of variance for sums of independent random variables, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot ({\\sigma }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+⋯+{\\sigma }^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot n{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is, it has mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1065 6276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "58763c8a-23b3-55bb-9b5c-4781a3c271b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1064 "The standard deviation of the sample mean is called the **standard error** of the sample mean and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}]\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]}\n$$\n\nSo, if we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the  standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1065 6300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c2217692-f38f-597c-9295-cd25f1d4d026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1067 "The Standard Error" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample ![](images/ff/ff40ddf134336ef15bcaf81b5b23aa8fb7c5ea13276e0f86fa6dde15841e0c5d.svg) of size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) from a population with mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) and variance ![](images/1a/1af459c3f0307649888db8b148eef968a9c5da6a77788c4ab7fe32d6ea30717e.svg) We know that the sample mean \n\n![](images/51/513b88bd8fa93336cc949758da79cac238f871b477d1682ab6b20635a4149361.svg)\n\nis an unbiased estimator of ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) \n\nHowever, it should be noted that selecting a single observation ![](images/3c/3c8b064868e0622effd6b83427f453ecdb3e033319c9e02969bb988b22ec7cb6.svg) to represent the whole could *also* be considered an unbiased estimator of the population mean. Recall that, by assumption\n\n![](images/e7/e7cc0631f5e6b060f0b9fc77dcac428c2b853d0e19be2e9b20e6e9f8d5d7aaad.svg)\n\nIn what sense is ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) a \"better\" estimator of ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) than, say, ![](images/c3/c3bb2b6f3769acd8b9c9ce9f929b5bc6c7ec840ab18503cf4c878a1fe8f8f41d.svg) The answer lies in their respective variances. \n\nAs the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) becomes larger and larger, the variance and standard error of ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) become smaller and smaller. It is easy to see that, as ![](images/f4/f4d85c2aac179db9322aef2478cfa080e9d602f8aeb8c0436f646f0e2321c139.svg)\n we have\n![](images/d5/d5676c70b4fcb7f68704ba0bb46fc262c7d8f764748e252aa989d6e82e88283b.svg)\n\nSo, when the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is large, there is less variance in the sampling distribution of ![](images/dc/dc01b2699b68bb8be585039efc249eaeeba5e4ad17a5c687a8811bf266cabc87.svg) Therefore, the larger the sample, the more likely it is that ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) will give good estimates for ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) The individual sample elements do not have this property since ![](images/63/631e2156cfdd9541694e1238fb8c717e721e0ef452de4bb898beb2c4509abc6b.svg)\n\nThe key takeaway is that the sample mean ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) becomes a better estimator for the corresponding population mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) as the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) increases." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1065 6301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2fc54f89-7780-558c-8193-28d92a025bc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sample Mean and Large Samples" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171525 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171525 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171525 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171525 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171525 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171525 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171525 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171525 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171525 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0a88d5f4-d750-5f16-a3bb-b2420ae3e78d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171555 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171555 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171555 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171555 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171555 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa2448a5-0101-5a28-982e-8f121a6f37a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Position Function of an Object Moving With Circular Motion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172044 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eb1745c5-6485-5a9d-9e8f-19492928e4b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172045 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186414225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172046 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172168 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172168 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172168 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172168 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172168 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172168 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172168 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172168 #edb/ref 1045 "Circular Motion About the Origin" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172168 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172168 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172168 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172168 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "1cf8d435-d537-5b08-8592-19448de0ebaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172650 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172650 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172650 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172650 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $2\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(5,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172650 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9927cf9d-99cc-5df6-888f-210e0aad32bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172650 #edb/ref 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(5,0),$ we have that $r(0)=5i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=5.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=5$ and $\\omega =2\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172908 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172908 #edb/ref 1061 31935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172908 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd3fa277-366a-51f9-a9dd-222993535dab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172908 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172913 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172913 #edb/ref 1061 32081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172913 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "392b768a-c4a0-5b11-884b-71eb53759374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172913 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172914 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172914 #edb/ref 1061 32082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172914 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1002b1a-1dd1-5a5c-addc-3f3958efbc84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172914 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172915 #edb/ref 1061 32083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172915 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3cf60778-9584-5fa1-a983-0b811161a21e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172915 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172999 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172999 #edb/ref 1061 35574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172999 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b328c2b2-b79d-5bf1-9c7d-2b99b0ab3382" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172999 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173094 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173094 #edb/ref 1061 45177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173094 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f5f2037c-c390-5f31-b9b4-856ee301062a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173094 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173162 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the unit circle configured in the usual way in the coordinate plane. Suppose a particle $P$ is initially at the point $(1,0)$ and moves counterclockwise along the unit circle with a fixed **angular velocity** of $\\dfrac\\pi3$ **radians per second.** \n\nThe first three seconds of the particle's motion are illustrated below.\n\n![](images/00/009b83d5d0e48c6983950a48523838cc569b3a92511dc9362630316b1d88f86b.png)\n\n    \n\nSince the angular velocity is constant, we refer to this as **uniform circular motion**. This fixed angular velocity is denoted $\\omega,$ and we write\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac\\pi 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.\n$$\n\nNow, let $\\mathbf r(t)$ be the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIt's worth building a picture of why this formula is true:\n\n- We know that $\\mathbf r(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf i + \\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf j$ traces out a unit circle, starting at the point $(1,0).$\n\n- We can see from the picture that $P$ will return to the starting position after $t=6$ seconds, so the angular frequency of the motion is\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ indeed returns to its starting location when $t=6{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(6) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,j \\\\  & =r(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if a particle moves counterclockwise along a circle of radius $R$ centered at $O$ with initial position $(R,0),$ then its position vector $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\\!\\,.\n$$\n\nNote that for clockwise motion, we have $\\omega < 0.$\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173162 #edb/ref 1065 5092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173162 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a0eebf3c-594c-5d34-868e-6c476f587498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173162 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173205 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose a particle $P$ moves along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\n    \n\nNote the following:\n\n- The **position vector** $\\mathbf r$ extends from the origin to $P$ in the *radial* direction, while\n\n- the **velocity vector** $\\mathbf v$ points in the direction of the particle's motion, *tangential* to the circle.\n\nFirst, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can easily derive a convenient formula relating $\\mathbf v$ and $\\omega$ by differentiating $\\mathbf r(t){:}$\n\nDifferentiating once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & ={r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={(R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j))}^{′} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the magnitude of this vector, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v|{|}^{2} & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t)^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t)}^{2}) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\omega t+{cos}^{2}⁡\\omega t) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v || = R|\\omega|.\n$$\n\nNote that while the magnitude of the velocity vector (i.e., the speed) is constant, its direction is continuously changing, which means that an object in uniform circular motion also experiences *acceleration* (more on this shortly)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173205 #edb/ref 1065 5805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173205 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "04a3ea53-9923-588e-b205-6980447b9231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173205 #edb/ref 1067 "Linear vs. Angular Velocity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173260 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's return to our particle $P$ moving along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$ plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\n    \n\nOnce again, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get the velocity vector $\\mathbf v{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf v(t) =R\\omega\\left(-\\sin\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\n$$\n\nDifferentiating once more, we get an expression for the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =R\\omega {(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,j)}^{′} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i-\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\omega }^{2}\\cdot R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\omega }^{2}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2 \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration vector acts in the *opposite* direction to $\\mathbf r.$ In other words, $\\mathbf a$ points *toward the center of the circle*. Moreover, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2 || \\mathbf r ||.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173260 #edb/ref 1065 7810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173260 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d24aa67f-988c-52f1-89ef-bb9571593fa1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173260 #edb/ref 1067 "The Acceleration Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179669 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179672 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921